WEBVTT

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:44.040
If there was ever a perfect example of how an invention dominates us, this is it, isn't it?

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:49.290
It's altered everything, from the way we organise work, to the way we scurry around,

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.660
to the way we let complete strangers into our ear when we'd never let them into our house.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:59.840
And above all, the world's economy only operates now because we can talk to each other across...

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:02.540
continents at virtually the speed of light.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.920
Without long-distance communication, the modern world would not function as it does.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:36.720
That's obvious.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:38.770
Take this equipment, for instance.

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:43.910
It's a receiving system in contact with a navigational satellite 600 miles up,

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:48.580
circling the Earth north-south so that as the Earth turns beneath it,

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.740
the satellite covers the entire globe.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.870
Now, as it comes over, it broadcasts two things.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:58.470
It says where it is, and it sends out a continuous note at a very precise frequency.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:01:59.970
Now, if you compare that...

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.630
note to the sound, say, of the whistle of a train. As the train comes towards you

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:14.090
and goes away, the note rises and then falls, like this. Now, the way the note

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:18.930
rises or falls depends on where you hear it from. If you knew exactly where

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:22.080
the train was, then what you were listening to would tell you where you

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.960
were, because you'd only hear it that way in that place. And that's what this

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:29.980
equipment does. There's the receiver locking in.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:39.640
the signal from the satellite. Now the computer is working out the one location on Earth where

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:45.050
a satellite at that particular point in space would give it the noise it's hearing. Okay,

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:56.240
here's where we are. North, 43 degrees, 42 minutes, 12.1 seconds. East, 4 degrees, 43 minutes,

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.960
18.8 seconds right you check this number

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.790
out on a map and this is where it says we are south of France near the town of

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:10.520
Arles at a position accurate to within 30 feet precisely there where it says

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:21.320
there is an ancient aqueduct there it is telecommunications can pinpoint

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:26.460
somebody like that did or because he picks up a telephone or because he's on

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:29.880
a computer data bank we organize ourselves better because

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:40.310
of that the question is how well organized will we become too well to a certain extent the modern

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:47.250
world would fall apart without that organizational ability the new community of nations that has grown

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:51.870
up from the bits and pieces of the old european empires the the french the english the dutch the

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:59.730
spanish portuguese is held together because we can organize but what will that organizational network

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.440
communications network? Do to us next. Well, the answer to that question may lie in the past,

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:13.040
because this kind of situation's happened before. The last time a world empire fell apart

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:26.520
was about 1,500 years ago. Then the empire was Roman. This is the accepted view of the fall of

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.920
Rome, you know, rape and village, destruction the way Hollywood does it.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.460
But what really let the barbarians walk all over the Romans

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.910
was something it won't take you a second to sympathise with.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:38.670
The taxes were too high.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:41.130
To pay for the army that was losing all the battles

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:43.110
and a bunch of freeloaders in government

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:46.300
and, of course, to pay for thousands of civil servants.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:50.070
So, for the Western Romans, better the barbarian you didn't know

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:51.730
than the tax collector you did.

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:54.000
So, the place fell apart.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:59.240
The imperial provinces cracked up into small barbarian kingdoms.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:03.480
And all that big-time stuff you have to have with imperial government,

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:07.660
you know, superhighways, theatres, aqueducts, were no longer worth the upkeep.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.360
That's why we're here, outside Arles.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:14.060
This aqueduct fed the biggest industrial complex in Europe with water

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:17.870
to run the wheels of the great grain mills at Barbagal.

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:23.150
28 tons of flour a day, a technological marvel perhaps to be lost forever in the chaos.

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:27.260
All through this period, the so-called Dark Ages,

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:29.620
the one organisation that still functioned internationally...

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.560
still travelled the Roman roads when nobody else would,

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:35.000
handling the king's local and foreign affairs

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.040
because its members could still read and write, was the church.

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:41.040
It had a fully operational network of communications

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.000
from bishop to bishop throughout Europe,

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.860
and that's what held things together.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:48.770
The church then was like our telecommunications now.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:53.020
And so the knowledge that the monks had accumulated gradually spread,

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:56.930
knowledge like how Barbagel had worked with the great water wheel

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:59.010
and the gearing system that made it so efficient.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.420
And in the end, by the middle ages,

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:03.480
look what they did with that wheel.

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:07.340
Here's the wheel being operated by water,

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.060
and here's the gearing system,

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:11.100
turning the horizontal movement vertical,

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:13.660
then horizontal again, and then vertical again

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:15.740
in order to operate the millstones.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.220
Here's another system operating a trip hammer

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:20.940
for bashing things like mineral ore or cloth or leather,

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:21.660
soften it up.

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:24.620
Here's a system that operates a similar trip hammer device,

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:27.470
but it's to work a suction pump for a water supply.

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:29.780
Same system again operating

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:36.780
levers pressing on bellows for a blast furnace and finally over here a crank that turns a circular

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:43.610
movement into back and forward movement for a sawmill beautiful system so put yourself in their

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:48.170
position the wars are all over there's loads of productive land everywhere you've got water coming

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:54.560
out of your ears and an amazing machine to use to harness the power what would you do yes you'd have

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:56.770
yourself a medieval industrial revolution.

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:07.490
The great thing about these wheels

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:08.890
was that they were easy to make

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:11.040
and they'd work almost anywhere.

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:14.080
You lived up a mountain, hollow a few trees out

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:16.340
and you had yourself a wooden aqueduct.

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:23.260
Horizontal wheels didn't need gears

00:07:23.260 --> 00:07:25.160
because they spun millstones directly above.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:27.770
You could turn a vertical wheel

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:29.970
with water falling from above or floating...

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.280
past below in a river and with gears you could slow down the effect of a fast

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:40.970
stream or speed up a slow one water power made you a lot of bread in both

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:48.850
senses but the star of the show was this the cam with a cam you can trip hammers

00:07:48.850 --> 00:07:57.700
to pound things with harder and faster than any human being and build yourself

00:07:57.700 --> 00:07:59.820
mills to work timber oil grain

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:04.610
leather, cloth, iron, beer, wire, sugar, coin, you name it.

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:32.330
It took a lot of energetic monks to get it all together.

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:35.700
Now, they were energetic because in 1098,

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:39.080
a bunch of Benedictines fed up with the luxury and the ritual

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:41.980
lit out for the wild country and the simple life.

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:46.160
And St. Benedict's original idea that hard work was good for the soul,

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:49.370
but it was the way these Cistercians organized themselves

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:52.170
that turned them into a medieval multinational

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:54.580
and gave Europe systems management.

00:08:55.630 --> 00:08:59.960
See, each monastery had to be self-sufficient.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:07.310
in food so they cut back on the praying and added six hours labor a day they went into rearing animals

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:11.930
and clearing and draining land they went out looking for new plants they could grow and they

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:18.070
wrote each other reports on the latest developments like this one growing vines on bad land hillside

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:29.650
terraces they used all the technology available wine presses water mills iron foundries a cistercian

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:29.930
Abby was like,

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.240
like a corporation with the special advantage that at the end of a hard day's business they

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:45.530
served the house wine in the company canteen. Mind you the food wasn't that hot, no meat,

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:51.900
they sold all that, just vegetables, nettle soup, a few roots, bread and silence while

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:55.630
you listen to instructive selections from the corporation handbook on getting spiritual

00:09:55.630 --> 00:09:59.100
and managerial strategy right, otherwise known as the rule of Saint Benedict.

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:11.130
Well, with this kind of organization, how could you fail?

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:15.140
Within a century, there were nearly 600 Cistercian monasteries.

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:27.190
These monks did everything with fanatical discipline.

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:28.570
Nothing got in the way.

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:29.830
No fancy architect.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.260
or ritual or colour to distract from the corporate image of efficiency.

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:36.510
And as their lands and their management techniques developed,

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:39.290
the news spread to the world outside.

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:50.640
Maybe their single biggest success was their sheep-rearing techniques,

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:54.160
because by the 13th century, they were producing the best wool in Europe.

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:59.840
So, there were the Europeans...

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:06.060
Europeans of the 12th century, with all that amazing water power technology and the red

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:12.320
hot industrial management systems worked out by the Cistercians, almost waiting for something

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:19.140
to happen, something that would generate enough money to trigger the economy off into high gear.

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:27.780
And when that something happened, it was one of those examples of the way change can come

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:29.730
about quite unexpectedly.

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:35.610
because the two inventions that were to trigger the great leap forward

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:38.550
could never have been foreseen here in Europe

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:40.430
because they came from China.

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:45.320
The Arabs brought them to us, and what a gift they were.

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:51.080
The first one of those Chinese gifts was a new loom,

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:53.330
and it immediately caused a problem.

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:57.400
It speeded up weaving because the thread-lifting business

00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:59.960
was now done by foot pedals, not by the...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.550
weaver's hands anymore. The new loom produced cloth so fast, they ran into the problem of not

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:11.780
enough yarn. See, up to then, you spun yarn in a way that hadn't changed for centuries. You teased

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:18.790
the fibres out of the mass and hand-twisted them onto a spindle. Took hours. Then, in the 13th

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:24.310
century, the second Chinese idea arrived and solved the problem because it produced yarn fast

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:29.980
enough to keep up with the new loom. It was the spinning wheel. Early on, they didn't have

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.860
much more than the wheel and the spindle foot pedals came later but these two

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:39.810
simple bits of machinery fitted together like bits of a jigsaw and when they did

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:51.900
the places they were used got very very rich places like Bruges

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:18.530
was one of the richest of the medieval cities built by the woolen trade and if you know anybody

00:13:18.530 --> 00:13:24.550
called draper boy were his ancestors well off the cloth merchants made so much loot they didn't know

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:29.840
what to do with it they built roads canals guild halls cathedrals they even had their own laws

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:35.150
And in spite of all that, they still had enough money left for high-technology music.

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:43.940
Not just this kind of toy.

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:47.710
The kind you can still hear in the cathedral towers all over Belgium,

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:50.340
where the Carrion still plays.

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:55.110
Recognise the mechanism?

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:59.980
It's the cam again, tripping levers that pulled wires that eventually pulled the cla...

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.800
on one or other of a number of differently-tuned bells.

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:09.000
You set the cams in like pegs to trip certain levers and ring certain bells.

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:14.780
Now, the reason all the good burgers had all these extra goodies

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:17.420
was because they'd found a new market for their wool.

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:26.440
See, all over Europe, people now had surplus,

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:29.020
and surplus always looks for a ready market.

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:34.090
South from Scandinavia and England and Flanders came fur and wool and cloth.

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:37.880
North through the Mediterranean, through Genoa and Venice,

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:39.740
came silk and spices from the Far East.

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:43.600
East from France and Spain came salt, wine and cordovan leather.

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:46.560
And from Russia, fur, I suppose,

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:49.780
everybody's crossroads lay in the county of Champagne

00:14:49.780 --> 00:14:53.880
at four little towns where they set up the first international markets

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:55.920
called the Champagne Fairs.

00:15:02.650 --> 00:15:06.030
The biggest fair was held at Troyes, in those days half the size of London.

00:15:06.710 --> 00:15:10.170
And merchants turned up because they got a special safe conduct from the king

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:12.010
and armed guards along the road.

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:14.330
Of course, the town made a bit out of it too.

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:18.410
You had to pay a license to set up your stall, and there was a sales tax.

00:15:18.670 --> 00:15:19.410
Isn't there always?

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:21.880
And you had to pay to come in and out of town.

00:15:22.660 --> 00:15:24.240
Not that any of this bothered the merchants.

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:25.760
They just upped the price.

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:27.800
Funny how some things don't change.

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:29.980
Anyway, this is...

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.680
International money-making went like a house on fire,

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:37.360
especially among those able to turn up with the very, very rare stuff,

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:41.010
like silk, where you really made a packet.

00:15:49.330 --> 00:15:54.040
Most of the really fancy stuff was brought by the Italians who practically ran the place.

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:59.660
By 1275, there were no less than 15 Italian cities who had consulates.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:10.990
here in Troyes. The reason the Italians mattered so much was because when everybody got back

00:16:10.990 --> 00:16:16.440
from a crusade in the Middle East to their rather dull northern European town, all they

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:21.870
could talk about were the amazing luxuries of the mysterious Orient. Silk, cinnamon,

00:16:22.540 --> 00:16:28.700
pepper, elephant tusks, things which the Italians were very well placed to provide at the fairs.

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:29.970
The Veneto...

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:34.930
The Haitians, the Genoese, and the Pisans all had trading colonies all around the Eastern Mediterranean,

00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:37.400
where they could pick up stuff from as far away as China.

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:40.760
Well, there was so much money to be made here.

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:45.940
And given the fact that the Genoese have always had a reputation for being where the profits are,

00:16:46.670 --> 00:16:53.640
it's not surprising that it was probably they who came up with a way to keep the financial ball rolling, so to speak, with this thing.

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:56.680
It's an investment contract called a Commenda.

00:16:57.100 --> 00:16:59.970
Now, this is a copy, but this particular one was...

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.540
written on the 14th of November 1244 and it's a contract between a traveling

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:10.520
merchant called John of the Parish of Saint Genesius and a draper called Otto

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:18.400
there's Otto who is investing 81 Genoese pounds as a share in a load of purple

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:21.200
cloth and gold silk that John the merchant is bringing up here to the

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:25.040
champagne fairs the agreement goes on to say that John can use his discretion

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:28.590
as to where and when he trades on condition that when he gets back to

00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:29.970
Genoa, Otto gets deep.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:36.120
accounts and his share of the profits. This tatty bit of paper which looks like

00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:38.500
an everyday affair thing you write in the back of an envelope practically

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:44.830
represent a really fundamental innovation because it brought everybody

00:17:44.830 --> 00:17:49.300
rich and poor who had any spare cash in on the act and that spread the risk and

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:54.510
that encouraged more merchants to go to more places. So the Champagne Fairs and

00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:57.580
others places like this really boomed.

00:18:06.940 --> 00:18:09.040
It looked as if good times were here to stay.

00:18:09.750 --> 00:18:17.120
And then, at the beginning of the 14th century, came a change in the weather.

00:18:17.890 --> 00:18:19.840
Freezing winters and rainy summers.

00:18:20.890 --> 00:18:23.080
Bad harvests followed, and then famine.

00:18:24.600 --> 00:18:29.970
With little or no surplus crops to sell, money became tight, and the fares began to...

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:37.480
All over Europe, people tightened their belts, and in this weakened condition, they were virtually defenseless against attack.

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:46.600
And when it came, in 1347, the effect was devastating, all the more so because they had no defense against the enemy.

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:48.530
It was a flea.

00:18:56.020 --> 00:18:59.940
The flea carried the Black Death, and from when it arrived in Europe in 1347,

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:06.370
on board ship from the Crimea to when it receded only four years later it killed maybe 40 million

00:19:06.370 --> 00:19:12.560
people. 200,000 villages were totally wiped out. At the height of the plague there weren't enough

00:19:12.560 --> 00:19:17.680
living to bury the dead. The flea sucked the disease in rat's blood and when the rat died

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:23.710
it jumped onto people and bit them. The effect was appalling from fever to abscesses in the

00:19:23.710 --> 00:19:29.780
groin and armpits to death inside 24 hours. Black pustules spread all over the body which was why

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:29.900
I think.

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:36.060
The effects were particularly bad in the towns, packed with people busy making all that money.

00:19:36.520 --> 00:19:49.050
The plague ripped through them, and a new face appeared in all the pictures.

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:54.230
And for those with itchy feet, a new kind of dance you could unexpectedly find yourself swinging to.

00:19:54.820 --> 00:19:55.840
The dance of death.

00:20:07.260 --> 00:20:09.720
One grimly enjoyable thing came out of it all.

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:12.500
The people who died left their money to the people who lived.

00:20:13.450 --> 00:20:16.210
All they could hope for was that they'd survive to enjoy it.

00:20:20.310 --> 00:20:22.150
Well, no nightmare lasts forever.

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:24.920
By 1351, the worst was over.

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:36.330
the survivors went insane, trying to forget the horror they'd lived through.

00:20:36.690 --> 00:20:40.670
Life, everywhere in Europe, became one long hysterical shindig.

00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:50.300
People spent the money the plague had given them on the wildest outfit they could buy.

00:20:50.910 --> 00:20:54.010
If you were rich, silk embroidered with gold wire was the thing.

00:20:54.740 --> 00:20:58.060
The middle classes went into expensive little numbers in wool and velvet.

00:20:58.470 --> 00:20:59.960
And the peasants? Wealth.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:05.020
Thanks to that loom way back and the fact that flax is cheap to grow, linen was their thing.

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:06.940
Well, it was everybody's thing, really.

00:21:07.190 --> 00:21:09.870
In hats and shirts and bedsheets.

00:21:10.270 --> 00:21:17.680
And especially if you take an indiscreet look up the nearest girl's skirt, that underwear.

00:21:18.490 --> 00:21:21.650
And just this once, that's the great historical trigger of change.

00:21:21.850 --> 00:21:25.180
What you're looking at now, yes, frilly knickers.

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:41.160
This is the first result of the great 14th century bed linen and underwear boom.

00:21:41.710 --> 00:21:46.630
The guy who used to go around collecting bones for fertilizer now started collecting linen, too.

00:21:46.960 --> 00:21:48.880
He became a rag and bone man.

00:21:49.590 --> 00:21:52.920
Why? Well, that's the second result of everybody wearing linen.

00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:55.520
Because when they wore it out, they threw it away.

00:21:56.000 --> 00:21:58.420
So there was this great pile of linen rag.

00:21:59.350 --> 00:21:59.970
And guess who went...

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:06.690
bananas about that. Okay, let me give you a clue. The first thing that happens to the

00:22:06.690 --> 00:22:11.290
linen in this process is that they take it and rip it against a knife to make the rags

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:18.780
even smaller. And what is shredded linen rag absolutely perfect for making? Yes, paper.

00:22:21.560 --> 00:22:27.570
So the papermakers got an unexpected linen rag bonanza, pounded by hammers, tripped again

00:22:27.570 --> 00:22:29.170
by the can.

00:22:35.920 --> 00:22:38.680
You bash the rag in water and gum for 48 hours,

00:22:38.740 --> 00:22:40.900
and the sludge you get is paper pulp.

00:22:41.540 --> 00:22:44.560
Slosh that onto a wire mesh frame, count five,

00:22:44.830 --> 00:22:46.310
and you've got yourself a sheet of paper.

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:48.020
Well, a sheet of very wet paper.

00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:51.920
So the next thing you do, at no prices, is dry it.

00:22:52.940 --> 00:22:54.840
Funny coincidence, the wire mesh frame.

00:22:55.100 --> 00:22:56.240
A lot of wire makers about,

00:22:56.540 --> 00:22:59.180
making all that gold embroidery people had started wearing.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:04.580
Anyway, the paper. You lay each sheet between layers of wool and cloth to soak up the moisture.

00:23:05.340 --> 00:23:06.940
Looks more like a sheet of porridge, doesn't it?

00:23:12.100 --> 00:23:16.140
And when you've got a big pile of wool and wet paper sandwiches stacked up, you call the lads.

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:25.180
All you do now is squeeze the pile in a press until you've got nearly all the water out of the paper,

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:28.260
when you hang it up to dry, and that's all there is to it.

00:23:29.300 --> 00:23:29.980
Funny how it all...

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:34.380
comes together here in the paper mill, the water power to run the cams tripping the hammers

00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:39.940
to make the pulp, the wine press come linen press to squeeze out the water, and thanks

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:44.620
to the automatic bloom, the linen that makes the pulp. And because of all that free linen,

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:47.820
suddenly the cheapest thing around was paper.

00:24:02.750 --> 00:24:07.300
This is one of those moments in history when things come together like a jigsaw

00:24:07.300 --> 00:24:09.940
to produce something entirely new.

00:24:10.770 --> 00:24:12.070
Look at the bits we've got so far.

00:24:12.400 --> 00:24:15.160
Because of the linen, we have cheap paper.

00:24:16.210 --> 00:24:17.470
The Black Death is just over.

00:24:17.720 --> 00:24:21.500
So the economy of Europe is on the up and up.

00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:23.420
Administration is expanding.

00:24:23.740 --> 00:24:26.100
There are many more clerks needed to do all the paperwork.

00:24:26.360 --> 00:24:29.080
However, the Black Death has killed half the clerks off.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:35.280
they cost a great deal so we have extremely cheap paper and the cost of a

00:24:35.280 --> 00:24:39.460
man who writes on it has gone up astronomically what do you need to solve

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:46.570
that problem yes printing and that's exactly what happened but before the

00:24:46.570 --> 00:24:50.250
final bit of the jigsaw could be put into place you needed one particular

00:24:50.250 --> 00:24:55.440
skill the kind of skill say a goldsmith has if you come upstairs with me I'll

00:24:55.440 --> 00:24:55.980
show you what I mean

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:06.570
You see, printing had been around for centuries, in the case of the Chinese, for a thousand years,

00:25:06.750 --> 00:25:08.750
but it was printing with blocks, like this.

00:25:10.750 --> 00:25:14.110
The trouble was, those blocks, being made of wood, would tend to wear down,

00:25:14.170 --> 00:25:15.910
and in any case, they only did the one thing.

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:22.090
Now, what our goldsmith friend did, and by the way, his name was Johann Gutenberg,

00:25:22.150 --> 00:25:25.030
and he lived in Mainz, in Germany, in the 1450s,

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:27.970
he used his expertise with precious metal.

00:25:28.500 --> 00:25:29.920
He knew what that was.

00:25:30.380 --> 00:25:36.110
the hallmark and he knew that the hallmark was made with a punch so he took a punch and he carved

00:25:36.110 --> 00:25:42.130
a letter on the end of it and using the punch he punched that letter into a soft copper bar then

00:25:42.130 --> 00:25:49.090
he designed a mold in two bits so that it comes apart you put the mold together like that you

00:25:49.620 --> 00:25:55.930
slide into the mold the letter you want to make any letter close it tight with a big spring turn

00:25:57.620 --> 00:25:59.540
it over and then

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:08.000
Very, very carefully, you put molten lead alloy into the mould like this.

00:26:10.260 --> 00:26:14.040
Leave it for just a few seconds, and then you break the mould,

00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:23.510
and the letter is there, ready to print with.

00:26:24.970 --> 00:26:28.210
And that letter A will go anywhere on the page you want to put a letter A.

00:26:28.310 --> 00:26:29.970
It will go in the place of any other letter A.

00:26:30.480 --> 00:26:32.880
The mould makes all the letters, so they're all the same size.

00:26:33.100 --> 00:26:35.220
It makes all the spaces, so they're all the same size.

00:26:35.340 --> 00:26:36.500
So the printing is uniform.

00:26:37.180 --> 00:26:39.020
But it's the interchangeability of the letters

00:26:39.020 --> 00:26:41.300
that is at the heart of Gutenberg's invention.

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:47.640
If you think about it, it was a good deal easier for a European to do

00:26:47.640 --> 00:26:48.960
than, say, for a Chinese,

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:52.460
because the Chinese language has thousands of characters,

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:54.340
and if you made every one of them,

00:26:54.420 --> 00:26:57.660
you'd need a space as big as this printing room in Antwerp

00:26:57.660 --> 00:26:58.600
just to store them in.

00:26:59.000 --> 00:26:59.980
Whereas the latter...

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:06.770
alphabet at the time only had 23 letters to be made as for the printing itself well that was a

00:27:06.770 --> 00:27:14.520
bit of a cinch this press was just an adaptation of a linen press that had been around for centuries

00:27:14.520 --> 00:27:26.000
as had the ink and the paper this is the first dated piece of printing we know of

00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:29.160
it's there may be earlier ones but this one has a date on it

00:27:30.380 --> 00:27:32.380
And the people who did it were very proud of what they'd done.

00:27:32.480 --> 00:27:35.280
It's the introduction to a book of Psalms.

00:27:35.760 --> 00:27:37.020
And the text says,

00:27:38.150 --> 00:27:41.690
This work, adorned with the magnificence of capital letters,

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:45.080
was fashioned with the use of a mechanical process

00:27:45.080 --> 00:27:47.900
for printing and making letters without the use of a pen.

00:27:48.730 --> 00:27:51.410
And then it says the name of the two men who were so proud of what they'd done,

00:27:51.510 --> 00:27:54.760
Joachim Forst and Peter Schoeffer.

00:27:55.390 --> 00:27:59.320
And then the date, 14th of August, 1457.

00:28:06.330 --> 00:28:08.830
The coming of the book changed everything.

00:28:09.880 --> 00:28:13.110
Perhaps the most obvious change was the appearance of the bookshop,

00:28:13.210 --> 00:28:15.030
where you could come in and buy anything you wanted to read.

00:28:16.170 --> 00:28:20.890
Knowledge was no longer the private property of the priest or the prince or the scholar.

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:25.400
If you could pay and you could read, it was all yours.

00:28:27.330 --> 00:28:29.720
The new book's also a standardised spelling.

00:28:30.600 --> 00:28:38.760
They carried an author's name, and they encouraged accuracy because the books could be widely read by people who knew more about the subject, perhaps, than the author himself.

00:28:40.410 --> 00:28:50.220
But perhaps most fundamental of all, the new books gave birth to the specialisation that is the blessing, or the bane, depending on your point of view, of our modern world.

00:28:50.770 --> 00:28:57.300
Because, you see, the architects and the engineers started to write about what they knew in terms that only their co-professionals would understand.

00:28:58.780 --> 00:28:59.980
The generation that first...

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:05.590
read these new books could as easily turn its hand to the lute or the sword of the architect's

00:29:05.590 --> 00:29:12.710
drawing and because of printing they were the last generation to be able to do that the coming of the

00:29:12.710 --> 00:29:17.850
books must have seemed as if it was going to turn the world upside down in the way it spread and

00:29:17.850 --> 00:29:25.410
and democratized knowledge and one of the few men responsible for that spread was an italian called

00:29:25.530 --> 00:29:27.670
Aldus Minutius, that he realised

00:29:27.670 --> 00:29:29.270
that what people needed and wanted

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:33.600
was cheap, standard books that they could carry with them

00:29:33.600 --> 00:29:34.900
anywhere they went in their saddlebags.

00:29:35.080 --> 00:29:37.560
And so he produced the world's first pocket edition.

00:29:38.380 --> 00:29:43.520
And he did so in what by 1500 was the printing capital of Europe, Venice.

00:30:01.560 --> 00:30:05.390
Used to blow their own trumpet a lot, the 16th-century Venetians.

00:30:05.910 --> 00:30:07.070
Well, you couldn't blame them.

00:30:07.440 --> 00:30:11.600
There were, after all, more millionaires per square inch here than anywhere else in Europe.

00:30:12.270 --> 00:30:16.090
Biggest navy, biggest overseas commercial empire, biggest bank balance.

00:30:16.410 --> 00:30:17.930
Venice was queen of the seas.

00:30:18.700 --> 00:30:20.580
Of course, there was nowhere else she could have been queen of.

00:30:21.190 --> 00:30:22.310
Not much land in Venice.

00:30:26.970 --> 00:30:29.980
She was a city full of businessmen, and because of her connections...

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:34.640
with Constantinople, she was also full of Greeks, refugees from when the Turks invaded

00:30:34.640 --> 00:30:40.170
it in 1453. And it was the Greek connection that gave the printer Aldus Minutius his

00:30:40.170 --> 00:30:53.010
big chance. Because Aldus got the Greek refugees to work for him, and because of that, his

00:30:53.010 --> 00:30:59.780
books gave the world a taste for the knowledge and the style of ancient Greece. He turned

00:30:59.780 --> 00:30:59.980
out...

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:04.620
dictionaries and grammar books first so his customers could learn Greek and then of course

00:31:04.620 --> 00:31:10.120
they could move on to reading the Greek books he would sell them. No fool he. Well, the new books

00:31:10.120 --> 00:31:15.460
got everybody turned on to matters ancient. One of the earliest bestsellers was a Roman thing on

00:31:15.460 --> 00:31:21.440
architecture that got people into big prestige building projects. People like Michelangelo.

00:31:31.660 --> 00:31:36.660
Thanks to Aldus and the Venetian printing presses, in 1500, only 50 years after Gutenberg,

00:31:36.960 --> 00:31:39.240
there were no less than 20 million books in existence.

00:31:40.180 --> 00:31:42.580
In 1515, Aldus died.

00:31:54.590 --> 00:31:59.310
Nutius was laid to rest with his books heaped around him as a mark of respect for what he'd done.

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:02.740
which was to print every major Greek classic in existence

00:32:02.740 --> 00:32:05.800
and invent a new kind of letter type for his pocket editions.

00:32:06.180 --> 00:32:08.960
It was a kind of print that would pack a lot into a tight space.

00:32:09.240 --> 00:32:10.520
We call it italic.

00:32:11.060 --> 00:32:13.300
So now the world could start worrying about something

00:32:13.300 --> 00:32:16.790
it had never had to worry about before, the small print.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:33.320
But above all, thanks to books, the world learnt about Greek science.

00:32:34.680 --> 00:32:37.120
This was one of the books that made the greatest impact of all

00:32:37.120 --> 00:32:38.720
by the Greek hero of Alexandria.

00:32:39.270 --> 00:32:43.090
It details how to make machines using the natural forces of air

00:32:43.090 --> 00:32:46.280
or steam or water as power sources.

00:32:46.460 --> 00:32:48.420
It's really talking about complicated toys.

00:32:48.860 --> 00:32:53.240
But this book and others like it put the world of Greek science

00:32:53.240 --> 00:32:55.960
and the ancient past into the hands of the armourers

00:32:55.960 --> 00:32:57.380
and the architects and the engineers

00:32:57.380 --> 00:32:59.980
working for the princes and bishops of 16th century.

00:33:00.580 --> 00:33:04.500
And look how the armourers immediately begin to work in the antique style.

00:33:04.760 --> 00:33:08.100
On this tapestry, this bunch of soldiers, they're using the latest in handguns,

00:33:08.180 --> 00:33:11.860
and yet they themselves are dressed like Caesar's centurions.

00:33:12.980 --> 00:33:17.610
As the wealth of the mysterious East and West began to pour into Europe,

00:33:17.800 --> 00:33:19.400
and the population began to soar,

00:33:19.780 --> 00:33:22.420
the princes also began to embellish their growing cities

00:33:22.420 --> 00:33:24.840
with elaborate water supply systems,

00:33:25.140 --> 00:33:29.900
operated by the same mechanical devices as were shown in the Greek and Roman books.

00:33:30.640 --> 00:33:34.200
And in their homes, the aristocrats would hang tapestries like this one,

00:33:34.300 --> 00:33:37.420
containing scenes of fantastic inventions like the flying throne,

00:33:37.420 --> 00:33:39.980
being carried into the sky by winged beasts

00:33:39.980 --> 00:33:42.900
that can never quite make the piece of ham above their heads.

00:33:43.370 --> 00:33:45.870
Or the mythical story of Alexander the Great,

00:33:45.870 --> 00:33:48.830
exploring the oceans on board a submarine.

00:33:49.780 --> 00:33:51.900
What the princes wanted were things, toys,

00:33:52.220 --> 00:33:54.580
that would show off their wealth and position

00:33:54.580 --> 00:33:57.280
in a way that would amuse and impress their friends.

00:33:57.600 --> 00:33:59.970
And now, their armourers and their...

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:05.390
engineers had the techniques to do it. One of the most famous armorers of the time, a fellow called

00:34:05.390 --> 00:34:10.720
Bartholomew Campy, switched, for example, from making this rather complex armor, a gauntlet,

00:34:10.960 --> 00:34:17.940
to making things like this. It's a clockwork tortoise carrying the god Poseidon, and it was

00:34:17.940 --> 00:34:25.530
used at the dinner table because they would set it down and it would take toothpicks from one guest

00:34:25.530 --> 00:34:29.970
to another around the table. The vogue for automatic machine...

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:34.460
1615's spread everywhere and with the help of the hydraulic engineers, it spread in a

00:34:34.460 --> 00:34:38.120
form that would bring people hundreds of miles just to take a look.

00:34:49.530 --> 00:34:54.530
This is one of the best ones still working, the castle of Helbrunn outside Salzburg, built

00:34:54.530 --> 00:34:59.550
in 1615 so the Prince Archbishop and his guests could have a little water-powered fun games.

00:35:00.860 --> 00:35:05.020
The whole place works on water turbines running the familiar cylinder with pegs in it,

00:35:05.320 --> 00:35:07.200
operating a 16th-century Disneyland.

00:35:07.730 --> 00:35:18.490
The name of the game was to get the most unexpected things

00:35:18.490 --> 00:35:21.150
to spurt water all over the suckers who come to dinner.

00:35:21.670 --> 00:35:24.150
Everybody laugh, ha-ha, because the host was a prince.

00:35:24.630 --> 00:35:27.090
And besides, you got a free meal out of it all.

00:35:27.490 --> 00:35:29.260
Well, that's not all you got out of it.

00:35:41.870 --> 00:35:46.460
Of course, ha ha, you couldn't get up until the prince did, and of course, ha ha, he didn't need to.

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:53.240
The craze for automatic machinery that spread through Europe came here, too, of course.

00:35:53.560 --> 00:35:57.500
Here, the pegged cylinders run an entire village of mechanical puppets,

00:35:57.620 --> 00:35:59.980
working like the Carrion in Belgium did on...

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:05.260
wires and levers. The whole thing's only 18 feet wide, and they packed 113 little people

00:36:05.260 --> 00:36:12.280
into that space. Over the top of all this water-powered wizardry, there was a mechanical

00:36:12.280 --> 00:36:18.230
organ to drown the machinery noise. And as you left, the prince would politely raise his

00:36:18.230 --> 00:36:18.550
hat.

00:36:34.030 --> 00:36:38.790
Mechanical organs and things might have stayed just that if it hadn't been for another craze sweeping Europe.

00:36:39.040 --> 00:36:41.700
A mania for Chinese fashions, particularly in dress.

00:36:42.020 --> 00:36:46.340
When, at the beginning of the 18th century, very complicated patterns became all the rage.

00:36:46.640 --> 00:36:49.680
Especially in France, and particularly in silk.

00:36:51.770 --> 00:36:58.330
By the beginning of the 18th century, the demand for this kind of pattern was giving the silk weavers of Lyon a real headache.

00:36:58.760 --> 00:36:59.980
Because silk weaving isn't just...

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:03.360
the simple over-and-under business of ordinary weaving. It's much more complicated.

00:37:03.720 --> 00:37:07.460
I mean, take a look at this. This already complicated pattern, if you follow it across,

00:37:07.980 --> 00:37:13.840
there, you see suddenly for about five threads that particular orange. So it comes in, say,

00:37:14.000 --> 00:37:20.730
at thread 530, and it disappears again at thread 535. Now, if you get that one thread wrong,

00:37:20.970 --> 00:37:26.440
you've blown it. Let me show you on this little model loom here how they cracked that problem.

00:37:27.560 --> 00:37:29.980
Every thread runs through...

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:35.400
tiny ring on a cord so that if you want to lift the thread you pull the cord up the thread lifts

00:37:35.400 --> 00:37:39.980
and in this case the crossing thread would go underneath and in the final pattern not be seen

00:37:40.070 --> 00:37:44.850
now if you tie together all the cords for all the threads that you want to lift

00:37:44.850 --> 00:37:56.000
into one bunch then one pull will lift them all like this now in a complicated pattern

00:37:56.000 --> 00:37:59.900
there would be a lot of those cords to pull and the children whose job it was to do

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:06.280
would get tired and pull the wrong chords and maybe ruin a week's work so in 1725 a lyonnais

00:38:06.280 --> 00:38:11.340
weaver called basil bouchon solved the problem because his father was an organ builder because

00:38:11.940 --> 00:38:18.150
his father used these things for his automated organs remember the organs used the same cylinder

00:38:18.150 --> 00:38:24.180
with pegs in it to make music as they'd used in belgium to work their bell ringing carry on and

00:38:24.180 --> 00:38:29.920
they'd originally got that idea from the cams set onto the shaft of the paper mill bouchon saw

00:38:30.160 --> 00:38:32.420
that the piece of paper that you give to the carpenter

00:38:32.420 --> 00:38:34.460
to tell him where to put these pegs on the cylinder

00:38:34.460 --> 00:38:38.350
was, in fact, a kind of control mechanism.

00:38:39.060 --> 00:38:40.060
So he put it on a loom.

00:38:41.150 --> 00:38:45.750
Look, each control cord comes over and down here,

00:38:46.340 --> 00:38:47.900
and whether or not it's moved

00:38:48.180 --> 00:38:50.660
depends on this horizontal needle here.

00:38:55.170 --> 00:38:56.890
OK, now for the control mechanism part.

00:38:58.330 --> 00:38:59.970
What Basile Bouchon did was...

00:39:00.440 --> 00:39:05.280
put a roll of perforated paper up against the needles, the cross needles,

00:39:05.600 --> 00:39:09.060
and where there was a hole, the needles stayed put, because they came through the holes.

00:39:09.300 --> 00:39:12.540
And where there was not a hole, as in the case of these four needles here,

00:39:13.160 --> 00:39:17.690
the paper pushed the cross needles so that all four needles and all their threads

00:39:17.690 --> 00:39:19.670
operated simultaneously like this.

00:39:25.640 --> 00:39:28.780
And to change the pattern, you simply moved the paper along one row of holes.

00:39:29.060 --> 00:39:29.980
But the paper...

00:39:30.030 --> 00:39:35.590
tore and the weaver has placed it in the wrong position. So around 1740, another weaver from

00:39:35.590 --> 00:39:42.170
Lyon called Falcon came up with this idea. He put each pattern on a separate card. Now the card was

00:39:42.170 --> 00:39:48.170
more durable and you couldn't really mistake how you should position it. Around 1750, one of the

00:39:48.170 --> 00:39:53.030
greatest machine makers of all time, a man called Vaucanson, who was also the inspector for silk

00:39:53.030 --> 00:39:59.070
factories, automated the entire process. He put the perforated roll around a cylinder and mounted

00:39:59.070 --> 00:39:59.890
of the cylinder on a chassis.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:02.180
which went backwards and forwards on water power, like this.

00:40:02.460 --> 00:40:07.700
And as it did so, it clicked forward one row of holes automatically each time.

00:40:09.210 --> 00:40:12.320
Now, that was limited to how much paper you could put around the cylinder,

00:40:12.560 --> 00:40:14.040
and it put men out of work.

00:40:14.270 --> 00:40:18.790
So, for nearly 50 years, this loom mouldered unnoticed here

00:40:18.790 --> 00:40:21.090
in the Paris Museum of Arts and Crafts.

00:40:21.170 --> 00:40:24.880
Until just after 1800, another weaver, who happened to be here at the time,

00:40:24.940 --> 00:40:25.920
was asked to put it together.

00:40:26.320 --> 00:40:28.080
And in doing so, he made a few changes.

00:40:28.100 --> 00:40:29.060
He put,

00:40:30.480 --> 00:40:33.400
together with Falcon's cards, and came up with this.

00:40:35.060 --> 00:40:37.390
It's automated, and it has the advantage

00:40:37.390 --> 00:40:38.850
that if you want to increase the pattern,

00:40:39.090 --> 00:40:40.570
you simply add more cards.

00:40:41.720 --> 00:40:44.060
Now, for that minor amendment, he got all the glory,

00:40:44.360 --> 00:40:48.480
because to this day, the entire concept is named after this man.

00:40:48.600 --> 00:40:52.140
This is a Jacquard Loom, and boy, what a success that was.

00:40:54.210 --> 00:40:57.270
Well, not in France, because the revolutionaries decided

00:40:57.270 --> 00:40:59.810
they didn't like fancy aristocratic patterns, but...

00:41:00.060 --> 00:41:06.570
in England where the loom ended up making things like paisley shawls very popular and where these

00:41:06.570 --> 00:41:12.410
cards got picked up for a very different reason they got used to control automatic riveting machines

00:41:12.410 --> 00:41:17.900
that by the mid-19th century helped to build the great new iron ships that were to make the

00:41:17.900 --> 00:41:23.390
crossing of the Atlantic safer and faster just in time to handle the biggest load of passengers

00:41:23.390 --> 00:41:25.510
that any shipping lines have ever carried,

00:41:26.150 --> 00:41:28.310
the poor, huddled masses of Europe.

00:41:32.940 --> 00:41:36.540
And though they didn't know it, these immigrants were to trigger off the development

00:41:36.540 --> 00:41:39.140
of one of the modern world's most extraordinary inventions.

00:41:40.030 --> 00:41:45.990
By the 1870s, the immigrants were stepping ashore on American soil

00:41:45.990 --> 00:41:48.230
at a rate of over 7,000 every day.

00:41:48.990 --> 00:41:53.230
The journey across the Atlantic had taken anything from 12 days to 3 weeks,

00:41:53.430 --> 00:41:57.680
and most of them travelled in conditions that varied from bad to appalling.

00:41:58.160 --> 00:41:59.980
Many of the bigger ships were designed with only one...

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:05.880
thing in mind to carry as many immigrants as possible and so they came

00:42:06.260 --> 00:42:12.530
in filth and degradation packed in like cattle treated much the same the vast

00:42:12.530 --> 00:42:18.110
majority came to New York at first to the immigration depot at Castle Garden and

00:42:18.110 --> 00:42:23.030
then later here to the place that was to become a symbol both of everything that

00:42:23.030 --> 00:42:28.720
America offered and the terrible fear that at the very gates of freedom they

00:42:29.030 --> 00:42:29.730
would be turned away

00:42:30.990 --> 00:42:32.830
here at Ellis Island.

00:42:42.880 --> 00:42:45.800
It took only a few hours to be accepted or rejected,

00:42:46.780 --> 00:42:49.950
and much of that time is spent confused and bewildered

00:42:49.950 --> 00:42:52.780
waiting, clutching their cardboard suitcases,

00:42:53.120 --> 00:42:56.210
tied up with string, everything they possessed.

00:42:57.210 --> 00:42:59.170
Some of them, those who could write,

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:19.290
even left their names on the walls as if to say, look, I made it. And then came the moment

00:43:19.290 --> 00:43:25.990
of truth, the point at which they either passed or failed the test to become American. What

00:43:25.990 --> 00:43:29.850
none of them could have known was how easy that test was. A quick look at the eyes.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:35.080
the hands and the throat and then the writing down of their particular details. The point at

00:43:35.080 --> 00:43:39.030
which many of them lost their old names because the inspectors couldn't spell them and they

00:43:39.030 --> 00:43:47.450
couldn't write them. So they became Smith, Brown, Joe. Eight out of ten people passed the test but

00:43:47.540 --> 00:43:52.720
with one inspector handling 500 people a day it was almost a case of if you could walk you were in.

00:43:55.730 --> 00:43:59.940
In the 30 years between 1850 and 1880 nearly eight million people got

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:08.720
And as the country grew and the frontiers pushed west, the immigrants were swallowed up to disappear in the vast open spaces of this enormous country.

00:44:09.220 --> 00:44:13.880
The trouble was, every ten years, the government had to find them all again for the national census.

00:44:14.820 --> 00:44:18.360
And as the population soared, the paperwork for doing that became unbelievable.

00:44:19.360 --> 00:44:28.280
And then, in 1880, an army surgeon called John Shaw Billings, who was working on the census, was watching the mountains of paperwork being shuffled,

00:44:28.320 --> 00:44:29.980
when he happened to mention to his young...

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:35.180
engineer assistant that he reckoned that the jacquard cards with their punched holes ought to

00:44:35.180 --> 00:44:39.200
be able to carry information you know if a man was married you'd punch a hole and if he wasn't

00:44:39.200 --> 00:44:46.180
he wouldn't the young engineer hermann hollereth worked on the idea and came up with this it's

00:44:47.460 --> 00:44:52.660
called a tabulator and it works on cards like these the size of a dollar bill of the period

00:44:52.660 --> 00:44:57.400
now hollereth chose that size because they already had holders for dollar bills and what

00:44:57.400 --> 00:44:59.620
that meant was he wouldn't have to design and build one himself

00:45:00.800 --> 00:45:09.500
No fool. So you put the card in here. Now let's say we're talking about a white male age 35 who is

00:45:09.500 --> 00:45:21.670
single, lives in Maine and came originally from Russia. Right. You punch white male 35 single.

00:45:24.390 --> 00:45:27.090
The code for the state Maine.

00:45:30.030 --> 00:45:32.320
And finally, Russia.

00:45:33.800 --> 00:45:38.380
Now you take the card out, see the little holes, and put it into this press.

00:45:39.020 --> 00:45:43.110
Now, when you push this press down, these little needles here with springs on them

00:45:43.520 --> 00:45:46.980
either go through a hole or they don't.

00:45:48.900 --> 00:45:49.700
Remember Jacquard?

00:45:50.600 --> 00:45:54.820
And if they do go through a hole, they make electrical contact down there.

00:45:56.720 --> 00:45:59.640
And that triggers these counters up here one click forward.

00:46:00.220 --> 00:46:03.060
Now, depending on what you want to count, you program the counters.

00:46:03.620 --> 00:46:05.380
Say you just want a general population figure,

00:46:05.540 --> 00:46:09.140
then all these are the states and territories of which that is Maine,

00:46:09.300 --> 00:46:11.280
and that one in the corner is the grand total.

00:46:12.430 --> 00:46:17.880
So our man in Maine would add one to Maine and one to the grand total, like this.

00:46:22.270 --> 00:46:23.530
And the bell told you you'd done it.

00:46:24.160 --> 00:46:27.960
Now, the census involved much more detailed analysis than that.

00:46:28.240 --> 00:46:29.980
So, Hollerith also...

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:35.180
designed a sorter this cabinet with lots of boxes in it connected to the tabulator. Now let's say

00:46:35.180 --> 00:46:40.920
you want to take a particular look at all 35 year old men. What you do is program the tabulator so

00:46:40.920 --> 00:46:46.520
that when one of them comes under the press it causes a particular box to flip open like this

00:46:46.520 --> 00:46:56.050
and you pop the card into the box and at the end of the day you took out all the 35 year olds

00:46:56.050 --> 00:46:59.980
and ran them back under the press to see where they all live.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:01.600
and to see how many of them there were.

00:47:02.070 --> 00:47:05.400
And you could do that with any bit of information on a card

00:47:05.400 --> 00:47:08.040
or any mixture of bits of information on a card.

00:47:09.440 --> 00:47:13.790
Well, the 1880 census had taken all over seven years to complete.

00:47:14.350 --> 00:47:18.230
With a new tabulator, the 1890 census was finished in half that time.

00:47:18.480 --> 00:47:19.680
And they checked the total twice.

00:47:20.520 --> 00:47:24.440
62,947,714.

00:47:36.080 --> 00:47:42.660
So, the trail has brought us from the waterwheel to the loom and the linen it produced that

00:47:42.660 --> 00:47:47.400
made paper so cheap it spurred the development of printing, of books that interested people

00:47:47.400 --> 00:47:53.000
in things like automated organs, whose pegged cylinders gave the French silk weavers the

00:47:53.000 --> 00:47:58.430
opportunity to run their looms with perforated cards that Hollerith used to count Americans

00:47:58.430 --> 00:47:59.970
who had once passed through this...

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:00.200
all.

00:48:00.540 --> 00:48:01.280
in Ellis Island.

00:48:02.710 --> 00:48:07.370
Gateway to the one country that more than any other would fall apart if it weren't for Hollerith's card.

00:48:08.150 --> 00:48:10.050
used to program the computers...

00:48:10.300 --> 00:48:13.500
without whose help the entire massive structure of the modern world

00:48:14.070 --> 00:48:14.670
would fall down.

00:48:16.660 --> 00:48:19.240
Most of the ancestors of the computer brought people pleasure.

00:48:19.890 --> 00:48:20.550
What will it do?

00:48:20.730 --> 00:48:21.390
Bring us.
